## `<parent>` — The Dependency Manager

Think of `<parent>` as a **"pre-configured settings file"** that your project inherits from.

### Without `<parent>` — you manage everything yourself
```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        <version>3.4.1</version>  ← you must specify version everywhere
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
        <version>3.4.1</version>  ← again manually
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.fasterxml.jackson.core</groupId>
        <artifactId>jackson-databind</artifactId>
        <version>2.16.1</version>  ← again manually
    </dependency>
    <!-- 50+ more dependencies all with manual versions -->
</dependencies>
```
This is painful — and version conflicts are a nightmare.

### With `<parent>` — versions managed automatically
```xml
<!-- You declare parent ONCE -->
<parent>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-parent</artifactId>
    <version>3.4.1</version>
    <relativePath/>
</parent>

<!-- Now NO versions needed anywhere -->
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-web</artifactId>
        <!-- ✅ no version needed — parent provides it -->
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-starter-test</artifactId>
        <scope>test</scope>
        <!-- ✅ no version needed — parent provides it -->
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

### What `<parent>` actually gives you
```
spring-boot-starter-parent provides:
│
├── Version management for 300+ libraries
│   ├── Spring Boot JARs
│   ├── Jackson (JSON)
│   ├── Hibernate
│   ├── JUnit
│   └── Logging (commons-logging, slf4j, logback)
│
├── Default Java compiler settings
│   └── encoding = UTF-8
│
├── Default Maven plugin versions
│   └── maven-compiler-plugin
│
└── Resource filtering
    └── application.properties auto-loaded
```

### Simple analogy
> `<parent>` is like a **company policy document**. Every employee (child project) automatically follows company rules (versions, settings) without writing them individually. If the company updates the policy (new Spring Boot version), everyone gets the update by changing just ONE line.

