## `<plugins>` — The Build Tools

Plugins are **tools that run during your build process**. They perform tasks like compiling, packaging, running, testing your app.

### The most important plugin for Spring Boot
```xml
<build>
    <plugins>
        <plugin>
            <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
            <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
        </plugin>
    </plugins>
</build>
```

### What this plugin does
```
Without plugin:
mvn package → creates a thin JAR
              only YOUR classes inside
              Spring Boot JARs NOT included
              → app won't run standalone ❌

With spring-boot-maven-plugin:
mvn package → creates a FAT JAR (uber JAR)
              YOUR classes inside
              ALL dependency JARs inside too
              → app runs anywhere with just:
              java -jar app.jar ✅
```

### Other common plugins
```xml
<plugins>
    <!-- Compiles your Java code -->
    <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
        <configuration>
            <source>17</source>
            <target>17</target>
        </configuration>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Runs tests -->
    <plugin>
        <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
        <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
    </plugin>

    <!-- Creates runnable Spring Boot JAR -->
    <plugin>
        <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
        <artifactId>spring-boot-maven-plugin</artifactId>
    </plugin>
</plugins>
```

### Simple analogy
> Plugins are like **power tools in a workshop**. Maven is the workshop, your code is the raw material. Plugins are the tools — one drills (compiler), one measures (tester), one packages (spring-boot-maven-plugin). Without the right tool, you can't finish the job.

